---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DiscoveryDiagram from '../components/DiscoveryDiagram.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../utils/paths';
---

<Base
  title="Research | James Young"
  description="James Young builds systems that map biological evidence, compare candidates, choose valuable experiments, and learn from each result."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <span class="page-path">ForetoData / research</span>
      <h1>Choosing better experiments in complex biological systems.</h1>
      <div class="page-intro">
        <span class="section-kicker">Map → Compare → Choose → Learn</span>
        <p>Biological discovery often means making expensive experimental choices with incomplete evidence. I build systems that clarify what is known, compare promising candidates, and identify the next experiment most likely to improve understanding.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell research-grid">
      <article class="editorial">
        <p class="lead-paragraph">The goal is not simply to rank biological candidates. It is to connect computational evidence to a sequence of experiments that steadily reduces uncertainty and improves the next choice.</p>

        <h2>Map the biological system</h2>
        <p>Nitrogen fixation is an unusually clear test of this approach. Nitrogenase is oxygen-sensitive, while cyanobacteria produce oxygen through photosynthesis. Understanding how some organisms coordinate those processes requires functional discovery across genes, regulatory context, environmental condition, and phenotype.</p>
        <p>I map the biological function, the conditions in which it matters, the available measurements, and the gaps that experiments must resolve. Oxygen-tolerant nitrogen fixation is the flagship application, but the same structure applies wherever biological function is undercharacterized and testing capacity is limited.</p>

        <h2>Compare the strongest candidates</h2>
        <p>No single data source settles the question. Comparative genomics, condition-specific transcriptomics and proteomics, protein-language-model representations, and other biological evidence each reveal part of the system. Combining them makes it possible to compare candidates while keeping the origin and limits of each signal visible.</p>

        <h2>Choose the next experiment</h2>
        <p>The practical output is a smaller, defensible set of candidates and a clear reason to test each one. Optimization helps balance predicted value, uncertainty, experimental cost, and the information a result could add—not just select the highest model score.</p>

        <h2>Learn from every result</h2>
        <p>Experimental evidence updates the model of the system and changes what should be tested next. Active learning turns that feedback into a continuing cycle, whether the question involves gene function, regulatory context, or protein properties and engineering.</p>

        <div class="text-links">
          <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('publications')}>Publications <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('work/oxic-nitrogen-fixation')}>Flagship case study <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </article>
      <div>
        <DiscoveryDiagram />
        <p class="status-note">Computational rankings support experimental prioritization. Experimental evidence remains the standard for biological validation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

